# Copy to .env and adjust as needed. Everything here is optional —
# `eren serve` works with no configuration at all.
#
# Settings are read as EREN_* first and, for installs from before the
# rename, under their old AICHIP_* names second: when both are set, EREN_*
# wins. `eren serve` logs each old name still in use at boot. Rename them
# when you can; the old spellings are a compatibility window, not a promise.
#
# `eren serve` does not read this file itself: Docker Compose does, and you
# can load it into your shell with `set -a; . ./.env; set +a`.

# ── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point Eren at your own Postgres instead of its embedded one (which lives
# under ~/.eren/pgdata, with a generated password). Matches the defaults in
# docker-compose.yml.
#
# The role, password and database are still called aichip on purpose — the
# project's old name. The Postgres image creates its role and database only
# when its volume is new, so renaming the defaults would leave every existing
# install pointing at a role that does not exist. See the top of
# docker-compose.yml. They are names, not branding.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://aichip:aichip@localhost:5433/aichip

# Overrides for the Postgres in docker-compose.yml. The password is a
# well-known default: compose publishes it on 127.0.0.1 only, so keep it
# there, or change it.
# POSTGRES_USER=aichip
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=aichip
# POSTGRES_DB=aichip
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# ── Running Eren itself in Docker (docker compose --profile app) ─────────
# Only needed for the "app" profile. Read the "Running in Docker" section of
# README.md first — there are real trade-offs.
#
# A container has no keychain and no browser, so the ordinary login can't
# happen there. Generate a long-lived token on your own machine:
#
#     claude setup-token
#
# and paste it below. It is a credential: .env is gitignored, keep it that
# way, and revoke the token when you're done with it. Eren never sets it on
# anything it spawns; the container's environment carries it, and the
# Claude Code CLI reads it from there.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# The folder holding the projects you want agents to work on. Mounted at the
# same absolute path inside the container, because git worktrees store
# absolute paths. Use one parent directory, not your whole home.
# EREN_PROJECTS_DIR=/Users/you/code

# UID/GID the container runs as, so files agents write stay yours.
# UID=501
# GID=20

# The host port the container's dashboard is published on, and the host
# address it is published on: 127.0.0.1 (this machine only) unless you say
# otherwise. 0.0.0.0 publishes it to your network — then turn accounts on
# (`docker compose exec eren eren admin create --username <name>`) or set an
# access token, and list the address you use in EREN_ALLOWED_HOSTS.
# EREN_PORT=4820
# EREN_PUBLISH_IP=0.0.0.0

# Previews and container apps when Eren runs in Docker. Read "Previews in
# Docker" in README.md first: this hands the container the host's Docker
# socket, which is root on the host for every agent run in it.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.previews.yml
#
# The socket's path and group (Docker Desktop and OrbStack: the defaults; on
# Linux, EREN_DOCKER_GID=$(stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock)).
# EREN_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock
# EREN_DOCKER_GID=0
#
# Where Eren connects to a preview (127.0.0.1 unless in a container, where
# the override sets host.docker.internal), and where Docker publishes one
# (loopback; a Linux host needs the bridge gateway, 172.17.0.1). Every
# interface is refused.
# EREN_PREVIEW_HOST=host.docker.internal
# EREN_PREVIEW_PUBLISH_IP=172.17.0.1

# The image scripts/docker-publish.sh pushes and scripts/docker-deploy.sh
# pulls, without a tag (default neiellcare71/eren). A name with no namespace,
# like plain eren, stays in the local Docker: publish loads it, deploy uses
# it without pulling. EREN_TAG picks a build (default latest); a commit's
# short hash pins one, which is also how you roll back.
# EREN_IMAGE=neiellcare71/eren
# EREN_TAG=latest

# ── Where Eren listens ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Loopback by default. 0.0.0.0 (every interface) lets other devices on your
# network in, each with the access link `eren serve` prints, and only under
# the names in EREN_ALLOWED_HOSTS (comma-separated; no ports or wildcards).
# EREN_BIND=0.0.0.0
# EREN_ALLOWED_HOSTS=192.168.1.20,mybox.local
#
# The token other devices need when Eren listens beyond loopback. Unset, Eren
# makes one and keeps it in ~/.eren/access_token (delete it to sign every
# device out). Set your own (16+ letters, digits, - _ . ~), or "off" for none.
# Compose sets "off" unless you set one: its port is published on 127.0.0.1
# by default. Once accounts are on (an admin exists — `eren admin create`),
# every browser signs in with its own account and the token is not consulted.
# EREN_ACCESS_TOKEN=off
#
# Only with EREN_ACCESS_TOKEN=off: acknowledges that anyone who can reach the
# port can use this machine's agents. The container image sets it, because
# there the port is only reachable through compose's 127.0.0.1 mapping.
# EREN_TRUST_NETWORK=1

# ── Behaviour ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many agent processes may run at once. Higher values burn through
# your subscription's rolling rate limits faster.
# EREN_MAX_CONCURRENT=2

# Serve a dashboard build from somewhere other than ./web/dist
# EREN_WEB_DIST=web/dist

# Restrict the folder browser to a directory other than $HOME.
# EREN_BROWSE_ROOT=/Users/you/code

# Where apps are created. Defaults to ~/.eren/apps.
# EREN_APPS_DIR=/Users/you/eren-apps

# RUST_LOG=info,sqlx=warn

# ── Engine binaries ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each engine is found on PATH under its usual name. Set one of these only
# when the binary is installed under another name or outside PATH.
# EREN_CODEX_BIN=codex
# EREN_GEMINI_BIN=gemini
# EREN_CURSOR_BIN=cursor-agent
# EREN_QWEN_BIN=qwen
# EREN_AMP_BIN=amp

# ── Object storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# For knowledge-base attachments (docker compose --profile storage up -d,
# which runs RustFS; any S3-compatible server works). Without these,
# articles still work; file uploads are refused with a message saying
# storage isn't configured.
#
# The access and secret keys are also the object store's root credentials
# in docker-compose.yml. Eren strips both from every process it starts.
# Upgrading an install from before the rename? Keep the values your old .env
# had, and a bucket named here is used as is.
EREN_S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9100
EREN_S3_ACCESS_KEY=eren
EREN_S3_SECRET_KEY=eren-dev-secret
EREN_S3_BUCKET=eren
# Defaults to us-east-1, which is what most S3 servers expect.
# EREN_S3_REGION=us-east-1

# Host ports for the object store's API and console (127.0.0.1 only).
# The old MINIO_PORT / MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT names are still read.
# S3_PORT=9100
# S3_CONSOLE_PORT=9101
